# Portainer safety

## Gate

Load on every Portainer turn. Skip only when the turn never touches
Portainer endpoints, stacks, or containers.

## Read-only default

List endpoints, stacks, and containers are fine when Portainer work is
authorized. Do **not**:

- deploy / update / delete a stack
- stop / kill / restart / remove a container
- prune volumes or images that drop data
- change endpoint credentials, TLS trust, or access control
- widen team/RBAC on an endpoint or stack

…unless the user gave an **explicit verb this turn naming the object**.

## Mutate gates

| Action | Require |
| --- | --- |
| Deploy / update stack | deploy / update / apply + stack name + endpoint |
| Delete stack | delete / remove / destroy + stack name |
| Stop / restart / kill container | matching verb + container name |
| Remove container | remove / delete + container name |
| Prune volumes / images | prune / wipe / purge + scope (unused vs all) |
| Endpoint ACL / credentials | explicit change verb + endpoint |

Prefer the narrowest action that answers the ask:

1. Named container stop/restart over stack teardown
2. Named stack update over delete+redeploy
3. Unused prune over "prune everything"

Ambiguous mass-delete or prune → **ask once** to scope before acting.

## Refuse secrets

Do **not** paste into chat:

- Portainer API tokens, JWTs, or session cookies
- Stack env secret values, `.env` bodies, or registry passwords
- Endpoint credential material

Prefer: endpoint/stack/container **names**, status, and "key is set"
language. Redact secret-looking inspect/log lines.

## No invented MCP tools

No Portainer MCP was discovered in the live harness catalog. Do not invent
MCP tool names for deploy/delete. Use API/UI paths the operator authorized.
If an MCP appears later, use only tools on the live map and still apply
these gates.

## Operator receipt (mutate)

Safe shape:

- Base URL product host (no credentials)
- Endpoint name/id
- Stack and/or container name
- Action taken
- "No API tokens or env secrets shown"

